*Automated scanning*

* *Scan for known vulnerabilities* — Detect known vulnerabilities across applications, hosts, and devices before release.
* *Manage 3rd party vulnerabilities* — Identify, assess, and address security vulnerabilities in third-party components using SBOM-based practices.
* *Manage 3rd party software updates* — Track and apply security updates from suppliers for dependent components.

*Security testing*

* *Test security requirements* — Confirm that security requirements are implemented correctly, including error handling validation.
* *Test threat mitigations* — Attempt to bypass mitigations to verify they work as designed.
* *Test input validation* — Verify boundary, edge, and schema checks on all external interfaces.
* *Test error handling* — Ensure the product handles exceptional and fatal error conditions safely.

*Environment protection*

* *Protect environments* — Secure development, test, staging, and production environments with access control, hardening, and monitoring.
====

=== Why it matters

Design and implementation controls reduce the likelihood of vulnerabilities, but verification provides the evidence. Without testing, you cannot demonstrate that security measures actually work — to your own team, to auditors, or to customers.

